{"complete": 1, "cvss": null, "cvss_vector": null, "cwe": [], "description": "PHP treats unknown methods such as \"PoSt\" as a GET request, which could allow attackers to intended access restrictions if PHP is running on a server that passes on all methods, such as Apache httpd 2.0, as demonstrated using a Limit directive.  NOTE: this issue has been disputed by the Apache security team, saying \"It is by design that PHP allows scripts to process any request method.  A script which does not explicitly verify the request method will hence be processed as normal for arbitrary methods.  It is therefore expected behaviour that one cannot implement per-method access control using the Apache configuration alone, which is the assumption made in this report.", "discovered_at": "2026-08-06T12:02:13+00:00", "distrust_reason": null, "distrusted": 0, "enrich_state": "mitre", "fetched_at": "2026-08-06T11:59:13+00:00", "fixed_raw": {"pending_nvd": true, "reason": "MITRE-published; NVD not yet enriched", "source": "mitre"}, "flaw_type": "cve", "id": "CVE-2003-0249", "policy_version": "2026-08-06.2", "published": "2006-02-27", "ref_tags": [], "refs": ["http://www.idefense.com/intelligence/vulnerabilities/display.php?id=97"], "severity": null, "source": "mitre"}
